            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Retention Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                ```
                <p>
                    Customer retention can become difficult when organizations do not
                    understand the reasons behind customer inactivity or churn.
                    Identifying the underlying causes allows product teams to address
                    problems before they affect a larger customer base.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <b>Poor Customer Onboarding:</b> Customers may leave when they
                    cannot quickly understand the product or reach its core value.
                    Simplifying onboarding and guiding customers toward important
                    actions can improve early retention.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <b>Low Product Engagement:</b> Customers who rarely use important
                    product capabilities may be at greater risk of becoming inactive.
                    Improving usability, education, and product discovery can increase
                    meaningful engagement.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <b>Unclear Product Value:</b> Customers may not recognize the
                    benefits of the product if its value is not clearly communicated.
                    Product messaging and user experiences should connect features
                    with customer outcomes.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <b>High Churn:</b> Increasing churn can indicate problems with
                    product experience, pricing, support, product-market fit, or
                    customer expectations. Churn analysis helps identify the areas
                    requiring attention.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <b>Lack of Personalization:</b> Different customer groups may have
                    different needs and expectations. Segment-based experiences can
                    make engagement more relevant and valuable.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <b>Limited Customer Feedback:</b> Without regular feedback,
                    organizations may struggle to identify emerging problems.
                    Continuous customer research provides valuable insights for
                    retention improvement.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant retention challenges image here when available. *@
